# ===========================================================================
# .gitignore -- the repository's single ignore file.
#
# Purpose: name every path that a build, a test run, a dependency install or a
#   local developer environment PRODUCES, so that generated output, Terraform
#   state and local environment files can never be committed. It exists to keep
#   two classes of file out of history: artifacts that are reproducible from
#   source and therefore worthless in it, and files that carry secrets or
#   machine-local absolute paths and are therefore harmful in it.
#
# Source of truth: this file is the ONLY ignore file in the repository. No
#   nested .gitignore exists under services/, ui/, data-migration/ or infra/,
#   and none should be added.
#   Alternatives Considered: a per-package ignore file beside each build, which
#   is the conventional layout for a polyglot monorepo. Rejected because a rule
#   split across five files cannot be reviewed as one policy, and the failure it
#   invites is silent -- a state file or an .env under a directory whose ignore
#   file was never created is staged with no warning, and the cost of that
#   mistake is a published secret rather than a noisy diff.
#
# Scope: this file is one of exactly THREE pre-existing files the migration
#   modifies, alongside README.md and CONTRIBUTING.md. Everything above the
#   first titled block below predates the migration and is left byte-identical;
#   every block after it was added by the migration and states its own reason.
#
# Assumptions: every rule here names a GENERATED path, so no rule can mask a
#   source file. That narrowness is the standing policy, and it is what makes a
#   broad pattern such as `target/` or `node_modules/` safe to leave unanchored.
#   A rule that could match authored source belongs in .git/info/exclude on the
#   machine that needs it, not here.
#
# Trade-offs: the file is ordered by ORIGIN -- pre-migration rules, then the
#   COBOL test-suite outputs, then the per-language build outputs -- rather than
#   alphabetically. Alphabetical order would make a single rule quicker to find
#   and would destroy the only thing this file can otherwise explain: which tool
#   produces the path a rule names. Each block therefore carries a heading
#   naming its producer, and a reader looking for one rule reads its neighbours.
# ===========================================================================

mock-data-generator/node_modules/*
sample/*
.vscode/*
mock-data-generator/markers/*
batch-app/markers/*
/.idea/
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CardDemo test-suite generated outputs, produced by scripts/build_test_*.sh and
# scripts/run_*.sh.
# Assumptions: this file names only GENERATED artifacts, so no rule in it can mask a
#   real source file. That narrowness is the policy for every block below too.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiled COBOL programs and shared modules (CARDDEMO_BUILD_DIR default).
/build/
# JUnit XML and coverage reports (CARDDEMO_REPORTS_DIR default).
/reports/
# Report and scan directories the migration workflows create IN THE WORKSPACE:
# .github/workflows/services-ci.yml creates data-migration-reports/ before the ETL
# pytest run, .github/workflows/infra-ci.yml creates infra-reports/ and
# secret-scan/ before the policy and secret scans, and .github/workflows/ui-ci.yml
# creates ui-reports/ before the Vitest run that writes vitest.xml into it. Running
# those documented commands locally therefore leaves four untracked directories behind.
# Assumptions: each holds only generated output, so no rule here can mask a source
#   file -- the same narrowness the block above states.
# WHY : secret-scan/ is the consequential one. The workflow populates it by COPYING
#   every tracked non-reference file into it, so an accidental `git add .` would
#   commit a second copy of the entire migration tree under a new path. The other three
#   would commit machine-specific JUnit, SARIF and policy-scan output whose contents
#   change on every run.
# WHY : Refactoring Rationale: ui-reports/ was the one directory of the four this block
#   did not name, and its absence was not visible from here -- the rule set read as
#   complete. `git check-ignore -v ui-reports/vitest.xml` exited 1, so a developer who
#   ran the workflow's own JUnit command locally was left with a stageable directory
#   holding a one-megabyte report whose every `testsuite` element carries the RUNNER's
#   hostname, a wall-clock timestamp and a per-test duration: values that differ on
#   every machine and every run, so the file is worthless in history and noisy in every
#   later diff.
#   Assumptions: the anchored form is used, as for the other three, because each
#   directory is created at the repository ROOT -- ui-ci.yml writes ../ui-reports from
#   its `ui` working directory -- so an unanchored rule would additionally ignore any
#   same-named directory nested anywhere in the tree.
#   Trade-offs: the rule is asserted in CI rather than trusted, by the check-ignore step
#   in ui-ci.yml's build job, so deleting this line fails a run instead of quietly
#   restoring the committable state it was added to remove.
/data-migration-reports/
/infra-reports/
/secret-scan/
/ui-reports/
# gcov artifacts from the GnuCOBOL-transpiled C (--coverage build).
# Assumptions: kept global rather than scoped to /build/, so a manual gcov run in a
#   source tree is covered too. The mutable per-run test workspace and its loaded VSAM
#   index files need no rule at all: scripts/test_env.sh creates them under $TMPDIR,
#   outside the repository.
*.gcno
*.gcda
*.gcov
# Python-harness coverage data (coverage.py; parallel mode writes .coverage.*).
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
# pytest and Python bytecode caches. Unanchored, so they also cover data-migration/.
.pytest_cache/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CardDemo AWS migration -- generated build output, IaC state, local env.
# The migration adds four build trees (services/, ui/, data-migration/, infra/) whose
# toolchains all write generated bytes back into the working tree.
# Assumptions: this set is intentionally NARROW, naming only generated artifacts and
# secret-bearing local files, so it cannot mask a real source file. The state and env
# rules carry the extra weight, because Terraform state records the resolved value of
# every attribute it manages -- including any marked sensitive -- in cleartext, and
# nothing encrypts it inside the working tree.
#
# Two kinds of value reach state here, and neither is a credential:
# (1) Never in state, by construction. The per-service database credentials come from an
# `ephemeral "random_password"` written through `secret_string_wo`, so the provider
# receives them and Terraform records only a version number. The Aurora master credential
# is created and held by RDS under `manage_master_user_password`. The Cognito seed-user
# passwords are produced out of band and written straight into Secrets Manager. And no
# TLS private key reaches state either: no `tls_private_key` resource and no `tls`
# provider requirement remains in `infra/`, because each task mints its own listener
# material at startup instead of being handed a managed PEM.
# (2) Not secret but not harmless: every resource identifier, ARN, endpoint and network
# address in the environment. State and a saved plan carry all of them, and together they
# are a map of the deployment -- which is why the rules below refuse them regardless.
#
# Refactoring Rationale: this block used to name an imported HTTPS private key, and then a
# GENERATED one, as the concrete state-bearing value. Both are stale: the two PEM inputs
# were withdrawn from both roots, and the generating resource was withdrawn after them, so
# there is no key material in state to warn about. Leaving either wording would have sent a
# reader looking for a variable or a resource that no longer exists.
#
# Assumptions: what these rules can carry, stated so none is mistaken for a control it
#   is not. An ignore rule keeps a path out of `git status` and out of an unqualified
#   `git add`, which removes the likeliest route to a committed state file: staging a
#   directory without reading it. It does not make committing one impossible -- `git
#   add -f` overrides any rule, and a rule added later does not govern an
#   ALREADY-TRACKED path. Accidental-staging protection is the whole of it.
# Trade-offs: the hard gate therefore lives elsewhere and cannot live here. The
#   no-secrets-in-the-repository constraint is met primarily by generating every
#   credential at apply time straight into Secrets Manager, by keeping the
#   per-environment tfvars limited to sizing and retention values, and by having
#   deployment authenticate through short-lived federated role assumption; the
#   remaining pre-merge gate is the gitleaks scan in
#   .github/workflows/infra-ci.yml, which inspects CONTENT and so still sees a
#   file that `git add -f` forced past every rule below.
#
# Refactoring Rationale: that last clause used to read that the scan "cannot be
#   bypassed", and it was not true when written. The scan stages every tracked
#   path outside app/, tests/, scripts/ and samples/, so its CONTENT reach was
#   never the problem -- but the workflow was triggered by an allow-list of
#   migration-owned paths, and a revision whose only changed file was a
#   force-added root credential matched none of them and started nothing. The
#   trigger is now an ignore-list of exactly those four reference trees, so any
#   other changed path starts the workflow and reaches the scan. The claim above
#   is therefore stated as what it is -- content inspection that a forced add
#   does not remove -- and it holds only while that trigger stays inverted.
# Assumptions: two conditions still sit outside every mechanism named here. A
#   secret pushed to a branch that is not `main` and never opened as a pull
#   request reaches no gate at all, and a secret already committed before a rule
#   or a trigger existed is not retroactively caught by either. Both are
#   properties of pre-merge gating rather than gaps in these rules.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Maven build output, one target/ per module under services/ plus one at the
# aggregator, where the Checkstyle gate bound to `validate` writes its result files.
# Assumptions: deliberately NOT root-anchored, unlike /build/ and /reports/ above,
#   because every target/ is nested under services/ and never at the repository root,
#   where a leading slash would match nothing.
target/

# npm dependency tree installed for the React SPA under ui/.
# Assumptions: the legacy `mock-data-generator/node_modules/*` rule at the top of this
#   file is PATH-SCOPED to a tree this repository no longer carries, so this unanchored
#   rule is required rather than redundant.
node_modules/

# Vite production bundle, written by `npm run build` to ui/dist/. Unanchored for the
# same reason as target/ above.
dist/

# setuptools distribution metadata, written to data-migration/src/carddemo_migration.egg-info/
# by the editable install that data-migration/README.md section 4 documents as a REQUIRED
# step -- `carddemo_migration` is a src-layout distribution, so pytest cannot import it
# until it is installed.
# Assumptions: the rule is needed even though `dist/` and `__pycache__/` are already
#   ignored, because setuptools writes this metadata BESIDE the package under src/ rather
#   than into a build directory, so neither existing rule reaches it. Without it every
#   operator who follows the documented install acquires an untracked directory holding a
#   generated file list and dependency set, which is build output that would then be one
#   `git add -A` away from being committed.
# Trade-offs: unanchored rather than scoped to data-migration/, matching target/ and
#   node_modules/ above, so it also covers a second distribution added later.
*.egg-info/

# setuptools intermediate build tree, written to data-migration/build/lib/ when a wheel or
# sdist is built from that package, which the migration runbook does.
# Assumptions: this rule is UNANCHORED and is therefore distinct from the `/build/` rule near
#   the top of this file, which is root-anchored on purpose because it names the COBOL
#   harness's compiled-program directory. Root-anchoring reaches only the repository root, so
#   it cannot cover a per-package build tree, and widening that existing rule instead of adding
#   this one would have silently changed what the COBOL rule means.
# Assumptions: verified before adding -- `git ls-files` reports NO tracked path anywhere in the
#   repository with a `build` directory component, so an unanchored rule cannot mask a real
#   source file. That is the narrowness policy every block in this file follows.
build/

# Provider and module cache created by `terraform init`.
# Alternatives Considered: ignoring all of infra/. Rejected outright -- the .tf source
#   under infra/ IS the deliverable, so a blanket rule would mask the very files the
#   migration exists to ship.
# Assumptions: the trailing slash matches the cache DIRECTORY only, which is what keeps
#   the sibling .terraform.lock.hcl tracked. That file pins provider checksums and is
#   what makes `terraform init` reproducible, so the slash is load-bearing.
.terraform/

# Built deployment packages for the operational Lambda functions.
# Assumptions: these are BUILD OUTPUT, produced from the reviewed sources beside them
#   by infra/lambda/build_packages.py. The sources are the deliverable; the archive is
#   a derived artifact, and each environment root reads it through
#   `aws_lambda_function.filename`.
# Alternatives Considered: committing the three archives, which would let a plan run
#   with no build step. Rejected because a committed archive hides reviewed source
#   behind an opaque binary -- a reader can no longer see what the function runs and a
#   diff cannot show a change to it. Every pipeline step that plans runs the builder,
#   and the builder is deterministic, so nothing about reproducibility depends on the
#   archive being tracked.
# Trade-offs: the directory name is matched, not the archives by extension. A bare
#   `*.zip` rule would also ignore any archive a future deliverable legitimately ships.
infra/lambda/dist/

# Terraform state, plus every backup and workspace-suffixed variant.
# Trade-offs: state records the resolved value of every attribute it manages, including
#   any marked sensitive, in cleartext, and nothing encrypts it inside the working tree.
#   This stack keeps its generated credentials outside that exposure -- per-service
#   secrets are written through `secret_string_wo`, the Aurora master credential is held
#   by RDS under `manage_master_user_password`, and Cognito seed-user passwords are
#   written straight into Secrets Manager -- so what state discloses is the
#   environment's whole resource inventory plus any non-write-only sensitive input an
#   operator supplies.
# Alternatives Considered: the single glob `*.tfstate*`. Rejected because its trailing
#   `*` spans the dot too, so it would also swallow unrelated names such as
#   `x.tfstateignore`. The explicit pair matches the state file and its dot-suffixed
#   variants and nothing else.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*

# Assumptions: *.tfvars is deliberately NOT ignored, against the usual Terraform
#   convention, because infra/envs/{dev,prod}/terraform.tfvars are required deliverables
#   carrying only sizing, capacity and retention parameters. Automatically loaded
#   override files are a different contract: .github/workflows/deploy.yml generates
#   deployment.auto.tfvars.json under infra/envs/<env>/ from resolved image digests and
#   secret ARNs and deletes it only on its success path, so a failed run leaves it
#   behind. These two narrow suffixes cannot match the tracked deliverables.
*.auto.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars.json

# Local environment overrides for the SPA and the ETL. Real values live only in Secrets
# Manager and Parameter Store and are read at startup.
# Trade-offs: `.env.*` is deliberately broader than the names Vite itself resolves, so
#   it also swallows `.env.bak`, `.env.prod` and any suffix someone invents. Enumerating
#   only `.env.local` and `.env.*.local` leaves a real hole -- Vite resolves
#   `.env.<mode>`, so `ui/.env.production` with a live endpoint in it would be staged by
#   `git add .` with nothing to stop it. Inverting the rule and naming the one tracked
#   file back in is what closes that permanently.
.env
.env.*

# Assumptions: both negations are LOAD-BEARING, not a forward-looking guard. `.env.*`
#   above matches `.env.example` directly, so removing either line would untrack a
#   required deliverable -- ui/.env.example documents variable NAMES with no values. The
#   first covers a template at the repository root, the second one at any depth.
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Private key material and credential files, which this repository never carries. Every
# key and every credential in the target is generated at apply time into Secrets Manager
# or issued by ACM, and the local runtime the setup notes describe keeps its TLS material
# and its role passwords outside the working tree entirely, so nothing legitimate has one
# of these names.
# Refactoring Rationale: this block did not exist, and its absence was the one real gap
#   in the accidental-staging protection above. The rules already here refuse build
#   output, state, saved plans and .env files -- the artifacts a tool WRITES -- and refuse
#   nothing that a person creates by hand. A key exported for a one-off test, an SSH
#   identity copied in to reach a bastion, or an AWS shared-credentials file dropped at
#   the root are all exactly the case `git add .` was going to stage, and none of them
#   matched a rule.
# Assumptions: none of these patterns can untrack a deliverable, and that was verified
#   rather than assumed -- `git ls-files` matched against every pattern below returns
#   nothing. The extension-less names are exact basenames, so `credentials` does not
#   reach data-migration/src/carddemo_migration/credentials.py or role_credentials.py,
#   and `id_ed25519` does not reach an `id_ed25519.pub` beside it. Public halves are
#   deliberately left unignored: a public key or a certificate is meant to be readable,
#   and ignoring one would only hide a file that is safe to commit.
# Trade-offs: *.pem is the one broad rule here, because PEM is a container format and the
#   name alone does not say whether the block inside is a private key or a certificate.
#   Refusing both is the wrong-by-a-little option and refusing neither is the
#   wrong-by-a-lot one, so a certificate that genuinely needs committing has to be named
#   past this rule explicitly, exactly as .env.example is named past the rule above.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
id_rsa
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
credentials
.netrc
_netrc

# Python virtual environments. The COBOL parity harness and the data-migration ETL
# share one interpreter created at the repository root.
# Assumptions: data-migration/requirements.txt instructs an operator to create it there
#   and states that these three names are already ignored at any depth, so the rules are
#   a cross-file contract rather than a convenience. Root-anchoring them would break it
#   for an operator who creates an interpreter under data-migration/ instead.
# Trade-offs: `env/` is the broadest of the three and would be a poor rule in a
#   repository that used env/ for configuration. The trailing slash confines it to
#   DIRECTORIES, and infra/envs/ is spelled with a trailing s, so neither is affected.
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Saved Terraform plan files, including $root/ecr.tfplan and $root/<env>.tfplan written
# by .github/workflows/deploy.yml, which removes them only when the run succeeds.
# Assumptions: a saved plan is not a diff summary -- it embeds the resolved values of
#   every attribute the apply will set alongside a copy of the prior state, so its
#   exposure is the same as a committed state file's. infra/README.md and
#   infra/bootstrap/README.md each give a `git check-ignore` invocation over *.tfplan
#   that a reader is told to run, so dropping the rule would turn a documented command
#   into a failure.
# Trade-offs: the second pattern is wider than the binary plan alone so that the
#   rendering from `terraform show -json <name>.tfplan` is caught too -- that rendering
#   is easier to read than the binary plan, so protecting only the harder target would
#   miss the easier one. Written as an explicit pair for the same reason as the state
#   rules above.
*.tfplan
*.tfplan.*

# Scratch sources written by the SPA documentation-gate probes in
# ui/documentationGate.test.ts. Each probe writes one deliberately
# non-conforming module here, lints it through the ESLint Node API against
# ui/eslint.config.js so the assertion measures the REAL gate, and removes the
# directory again in an afterEach that runs whatever the outcome.
# Assumptions: the path has to sit under ui/src, and that is a constraint rather
# than a preference -- ui/eslint.config.js gives the TypeScript parser an
# explicit parserOptions.project list, and ui/tsconfig.json's `include: ["src"]`
# is resolved against the real filesystem, so a probe written anywhere else is
# reported as belonging to no project and the run dies with a parser error before
# a single jsdoc rule is reached.
# Trade-offs: the directory should never exist when git is consulted, so this
# rule protects only against an aborted or interrupted run. It is carried anyway
# because the failure it prevents is a bad one and is easy to misdiagnose: a
# leftover probe under ui/src is by construction non-conforming, so the next
# `npm run lint` fails inside the gate's own package on a file nobody authored.
# Refactoring Rationale: not written as the wider `__*__/` glob. That would also
# swallow a future authored directory using the same dunder convention, and the
# point of naming this one path is that its contents are known to be disposable.
ui/src/__documentation_gate_probe__/

# Per-run browser-validation artifacts. The platform's headless-Chrome runner
# writes its screenshots and screen recordings into these two directories inside
# the working tree, which is how a runtime check reports what it saw -- a sign-on
# flow reaching the admin menu, a refusal rendering its catalogued sentence.
# Assumptions: `blitzy/documentation/` is deliberately NOT covered. That path
# holds an authored, tracked document, so the two evidence directories are named
# individually rather than ignoring `blitzy/` wholesale, which would silently
# untrack it.
# Trade-offs: the evidence is then not preserved by version control, and that is
# the right trade. A single run deposits tens of megabytes of PNG and WEBM whose
# value expires with the check that produced it, the runner is shared so a
# directory can hold another run's captures as well, and a binary artifact
# committed once stays in the history for good. What a run CONCLUDED belongs in
# its written report, which is text and is reviewable; what it LOOKED LIKE is
# disposable.
blitzy/screenshots/
blitzy/screen_recordings/

# WHY : Assumptions: the per-run evidence directory is named here for exactly the
# reason the two directories above are, and it is listed separately because it holds
# TEXT rather than binaries -- the logs, scratch inputs and deliberately corrupted
# fixtures a runtime check writes while it works. Being text made it look committable
# in a way a PNG never does, and a `git add -A` during a wide edit swept a hundred of
# them into the index; the rule closes that path rather than relying on the next
# person noticing. Trade-offs: identical to the two above. What a run CONCLUDED
# belongs in its written report; the scratch it produced on the way expires with the
# check, and one of those files is a corrupted extract that exists to be rejected.
blitzy/qa-evidence/
